{
  "difficulty": "intermediate",
  "score": 6,
  "estimated_minutes": 45,
  "factors": {
    "transaction_background": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "The reviewer must understand the 300-acre Wootten North Unit, Equinor’s wellhead sales to affiliate ENG, and the distinction between the defendants’ unitization motions and Equinor’s royalty motion."
    },
    "domain_knowledge": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "Proceeds royalties, wellhead versus downstream sales, net-back calculations, and the duty to market require industry-specific learning, but the opinion supplies the essential explanations."
    },
    "defect_spread": {
      "level": "high",
      "note": "The meaning of 'marketed and used off the premises' requires reading § 4(B), its deleted cost-deduction language, the § 6 shut-in provision, the § 14 operational-use provision, and the § 20 implied-obligation disclaimer together."
    },
    "court_reasoning": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "The reviewer must distinguish the definitive proceeds-price and density-ratio constructions from the unresolved applicability and express-marketing-duty questions that merely survive dismissal."
    },
    "issue_distinctness": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "The royalty-price and marketing-duty issues concern the same affiliate-sale practice but different contractual questions, while the density requirement’s applicability is separate from how its ratio operates."
    }
  },
  "hardest_defect": "d02 — Understanding the possible express marketing duty requires reconciling several provisions and the deleted cost language with the disclaimer of implied obligations. The reviewer must also recognize that the court accepted plaintiffs’ reading as reasonable at the pleading stage without finally adopting that duty or deciding the implied-covenant theory.",
  "reason": "Assign this case to a reviewer comfortable with cross-provision interpretation and distinguishing pleading-stage rulings from final constructions; oil-and-gas expertise is helpful but not essential. The principal challenge is explaining why a royalty based on proceeds actually received can coexist with a possible obligation governing how Equinor markets the gas. The unitization discussion is more accessible because the opinion clearly separates which units are covered from how many wells the density ratio requires. Focused reading of the relevant lease sections and the royalty analysis should produce confident understanding without extensive outside research."
}
